<article class="paper">

<div class="titleblock">
  <h1>Where the Heat Actually Is</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Decomposing actuator load in learned legged locomotion,<br>
    and the limits of command filtering</p>
  <p class="byline">Author <span class="pending">pending</span></p>
  <p class="affiliation">Affiliation <span class="pending">pending</span> &middot;
    Corresponding author <span class="pending">pending</span></p>
  <p class="stamp"><span>Preprint, draft v2</span>
    <span>10 September 2026</span>
    <span>Simulation only</span>
    <span>Unitree Go1, MuJoCo, policy frozen</span></p>
</div>

<div class="abstract">
<p class="noind"><span class="runin">Abstract&mdash;</span>Reinforcement-learning locomotion policies
frequently overheat commercial quadrupedal and humanoid actuators in operating regimes where vendor
controllers remain thermally stable. The standard explanation attributes this dissipation to
high-frequency command jitter and prescribes smoothness penalties in the reward function as the
default remedy. That hypothesis has not been tested against the alternative: that learned policies
adopt postures demanding higher steady-state torque. We separate the two mechanisms by showing that
electrical copper loss decomposes exactly into a posture term and a ripple term which add in
quadrature, so that their relative dominance is fixed by a joint's steady-state torque rather than by
the policy. We locate this crossover analytically and empirically at 3.2 N&middot;m on a Unitree Go1.
Load-bearing calf joints sit above it and carry only 14&ndash;17% of their heat in bands a filter can
reach; hip-abduction joints sit below it and carry 33&ndash;45%.</p>

<p>Evaluating post-hoc command filtering on frozen policies, a Parseval decomposition of joint torque
shows that zero-order-hold staircase harmonics account for 1.6% of total heat, while 5&ndash;25 Hz
command content carries the remaining discretionary dissipation. Open-loop replay suggests a 46%
thermal reduction is available through zero-phase filtering, but that ceiling collapses in closed
loop. Buying zero phase with 40 ms of latency degrades feedback stability, producing falls in 20% of
episodes at 1.0 m/s on a well-regularized policy and in every episode on an unregularized one. A
causal order-8 predictive filter with joint-load gating instead recovers 47% and 54% of the reachable
thermal budget on the two checkpoints without inducing falls. Finally, we show that the widely
deployed <code>action_rate</code> penalty does not govern command-side thermal load, whereas effort
and energy penalties set the reachable budget.</p>
</div>

<p class="terms noind"><b>Index terms&mdash;</b>legged locomotion, reinforcement learning, actuator
thermal load, copper loss, command smoothing, zero-order hold, spectral decomposition, closed-loop
stability.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><b>Status of the numbers on this page.</b> Quantities are taken from the v1 source draft
(<code>paper-command-filtering-draft.md</code>), which is authoritative wherever the two drafts differ.
Where v1's own tables and prose disagree, the table is used; Appendix&nbsp;B records every such ruling
and its basis. Quantities that exist in neither draft are printed as
<span class="pending">pending</span> and listed in Appendix&nbsp;C. No number on this page has been
inferred, interpolated, or reconstructed.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="secno">1.</span>Introduction</h2>

<p class="noind">Legged locomotion policies trained by reinforcement learning routinely reach actuator
thermal limits in deployment. On a Unitree G1 humanoid running a whole-body controller, ankle-roll
actuators reach roughly 90&nbsp;&deg;C within five to ten minutes of static standing, while the
manufacturer's own controller holds the identical joints between 30 and 45&nbsp;&deg;C.<a class="cite"
href="#ref1">[1]</a> On a Unitree A1 carrying a 3 kg payload, baseline policies trip thermal
protection in five to seven minutes.<a class="cite" href="#ref2">[2]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref3">,
[3]</a> The relation is not one-directional: on a Go2-W a learned policy plateaus at
60&nbsp;&deg;C over 51 minutes of walking while the vendor controller diverges to 84&nbsp;&deg;C and
shuts down at 34 minutes.<a class="cite" href="#ref4">[4]</a> Policy behaviour clearly governs
dissipation. Which behaviour is a matter of open disagreement.</p>

<p>Two hypotheses are in circulation. Under the <em>posture hypothesis</em>, dissipation is set by the
steady holding torque the chosen kinematic posture demands; the Go2-W trials support this, attributing
a 24&nbsp;&deg;C difference at 50 Hz to periodic foot lifting that relieved steady joint
torque.<a class="cite" href="#ref4">[4]</a> Under the <em>jitter hypothesis</em>, the excess comes from
high-frequency command oscillation that inflates RMS current without producing useful torque; the G1
ankle-roll failure supports this, since static holding torque there is fixed by gravity and yet two
controllers differ by 45&ndash;60 K.<a class="cite" href="#ref1">[1]</a></p>

<p>Neither hypothesis has been tested by measuring both terms independently. Copper loss requires that
posture torque and dynamic torque ripple add in quadrature:</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H} \;\propto\; \mathbb{E}[\tau^2] \;=\; \underbrace{\bar{\tau}^2}_{\text{posture}} \;+\; \underbrace{\mathrm{Var}(\tau)}_{\text{ripple}} .
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">A zero-mean command ripple produces no net torque and contributes its full variance to
dissipation. Because the two terms add in quadrature, a large steady torque \(\bar{\tau}\) suppresses
the <em>relative</em> contribution of \(\mathrm{Var}(\tau)\). Whether command jitter governs thermal
load is therefore a property of joint-specific loading, not an intrinsic property of the policy. Both
camps can be, and we argue are, describing different joints.</p>

<p>This paper locates the crossover between the two regimes, quantifies how much thermal recovery
command filtering can deliver on a frozen policy, and identifies the closed-loop stability boundary
that governs whether such a filter can be deployed at all.</p>

<h3><span class="secno"></span>Contributions</h3>

<ol class="contrib">
<li><b>Spectral heat decomposition.</b> An exact Parseval-based split of joint torque into steady
posture, gait-synchronous torque, in-band jitter (5&ndash;25 Hz), and zero-order-hold discretization
harmonics (&gt; 25 Hz). The reachable budget is 18.1% for a well-regularized Go1 policy and 43.3% for a
policy trained without effort penalties, with ZOH harmonics accounting for 1.6%.</li>
<li><b>Thermal load crossover.</b> Out-of-band heating follows
\(\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_0 + \kappa\rho/(1-\rho)\) to within 0.45% residual. Posture overtakes
ripple at \(\bar{\tau} \approx \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\tau)} \approx 3.2\) N&middot;m on a Go1. Joints
below this threshold carry two to three times the filter-reachable heat share of joints above it.</li>
<li><b>Closed-loop latency penalty.</b> The 46% open-loop ceiling collapses to an 8.5% closed-loop
reduction (Cliff's \(\delta = -0.33\), a small effect). Buying zero phase with 40 ms of latency
compromises feedback margins: 20% fall rate at 1.0 m/s on the regularized policy, 100% on the
unregularized one.</li>
<li><b>Predictive gated filtering.</b> An order-8 causal linear autoregressive filter recovers 47% and
54% of the filter-reachable budget on the regularized and unregularized policies respectively, with no
falls. Gating by joint load preserves tracking fidelity.</li>
<li><b>Reward ablation.</b> Ablating <code>action_rate</code> raises step transients by 8%; ablating
effort and energy penalties raises them by 89%. The penalty the field reaches for is not the one that
governs command-side thermal load.</li>
</ol>

