<h2><span class="secno">2.</span>Actuation Pipeline and Problem Formulation</h2>

<p class="noind">Commercial quadruped and humanoid architectures run proportional-derivative loops on
the motor drive rather than inside the policy process. The policy emits position targets at
\(f_{\text{pol}}\); these cross a communication bus to motor-side PD loops running at higher rate. On
Unitree systems the policy executes at 50 Hz and low-level commands are written at
500 Hz.<a class="cite" href="#ref62">[62]</a> DeepRobotics Lite3 deployments run a 1 kHz state
loop.<a class="cite" href="#ref63">[63]</a> Vendor datasheets give a communication control frequency
of 6 kHz for the Unitree GO-M8010-6<a class="cite" href="#ref6">[6]</a> and 1 kHz for the DeepRobotics
J60.<a class="cite" href="#ref61">[61]</a> The policy rate used in Lite3 deployments is
<span class="pending">pending</span>: an earlier draft put it near 83 Hz, but no public source states
that figure and the only published rate we could find is roughly 50 Hz.</p>

<p>A zero-order hold (ZOH) sits between policy and low-level loop. At 50 Hz into 500 Hz each command is
held for ten ticks and then steps discontinuously, producing an instantaneous proportional transient of
\(k_p \Delta q\) before the rotor has moved.</p>

<p>In standardized environments such as <code>unitree_rl_mjlab</code>, PD gains are derived from a
target closed-loop natural frequency of 10 Hz and a damping ratio \(\zeta = 2.0\), with action scaling
set to \(0.25\,e/k_p\) where \(e\) is the effort limit. An action reversal between successive policy
steps therefore commands a peak transient</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\text{step}} \;=\; k_p \cdot 2 \cdot \frac{0.25\,e}{k_p} \;=\; 0.5\,e .
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">A full-scale action reversal commands a transient equal to half the joint torque
limit. With \(\zeta = 2.0\) the dominant closed-loop pole sits at 2.68 Hz, while the 50 Hz command
stream carries content up to its 25 Hz Nyquist limit. The gap between those two numbers is the subject
of this paper.</p>

<h2><span class="secno">3.</span>Metrics</h2>

<h3><span class="secno">3.1</span>Spectral heat decomposition</h3>

<p class="noind">Applying Parseval's theorem to joint torque partitioned into mean and zero-mean
components gives</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\tau^2] \;=\; \bar\tau^2 \;+\; \int_{0}^{f_g} S_\tau(f)\,\mathrm{d}f \;+\; \int_{f_g}^{f_{\text{Nyq}}} S_\tau(f)\,\mathrm{d}f \;+\; \mathbb{E}_k\!\left[\mathrm{Var}_{\text{sub}}(\tau)\right] ,
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">where \(f_g = 5\) Hz isolates the fundamental gait frequencies, \(f_{\text{Nyq}} =
25\) Hz is the policy Nyquist frequency, and \(\mathbb{E}_k[\mathrm{Var}_{\text{sub}}(\tau)]\) is the
intra-step variance above Nyquist arising from ZOH discretization. The last two terms together define
the <strong>reachable share</strong>: the only part of the heat a command-stream filter can act on at
all.</p>

<h3><span class="secno">3.2</span>Ripple fraction and out-of-band command power</h3>

<p class="noind">When a power spectral density is unavailable we use the joint-level ripple fraction</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
r \;=\; \frac{\mathrm{Var}(\tau)}{\bar{\tau}^2 + \mathrm{Var}(\tau)} .
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">Command-side jitter is quantified independently of the plant by the out-of-band
command power fraction</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
\rho \;=\; \frac{\int_{f_c}^{f_{\text{Nyq}}} S_q(f)\,\mathrm{d}f}{\int_{0}^{f_{\text{Nyq}}} S_q(f)\,\mathrm{d}f} ,
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">where \(f_c\) is the joint's \(-3\) dB closed-loop bandwidth, 15.3 Hz for the Go1 model
of Section 4. Table 2 reports a second, coarser cut of the same quantity taken at 5 Hz; it is written
\(\rho_{>5\,\mathrm{Hz}}\) throughout and is not interchangeable with \(\rho\).</p>

<h3><span class="secno">3.3</span>Thermal proxy</h3>

<p class="noind">Motor thermal load is quantified as \(\mathcal{H} = \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T
\tau^2\,\mathrm{d}t\), proportional to \(I_{\text{rms}}^2 R\) under a constant torque coefficient.
Stator thermal dynamics justify squared rather than absolute torque: on calibrated brushless actuators
the winding thermal time constant is 3.12 s against 777 s for the whole motor housing, a factor of
249.<a class="cite" href="#ref7">[7]</a> Reported driver temperatures lag winding heat, which makes a
current-based proxy the relevant primary metric.<a class="cite" href="#ref8">[8]</a></p>

<h2><span class="secno">4.</span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<p class="noind">Four protocols are used. The labels are those of the source draft; there is no E4, as
the label was never assigned.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>E1 &mdash; single joint, synthetic commands.</strong> A second-order joint
modelled on the Go1 (\(k_p = 35\), damping 0.5, armature 0.005 kg&middot;m&sup2;, \(f_n = 13.3\) Hz,
\(\zeta = 0.60\), \(-3\) dB bandwidth 15.3 Hz) is driven by a 2 Hz sinusoidal command with bandpass
noise injected above bandwidth. Commands are sampled at 50 Hz, held by ZOH to 500 Hz, and integrated at
5 kHz. Constant load torques are swept from 0 to 20 N&middot;m.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>E2 &mdash; recorded commands, open loop.</strong> Trajectories recorded from
seven Go1 policies spanning a reward ablation are replayed through the single-joint model under
different filtering schemes. Preserved motion is benchmarked against the ZOH trajectory low-passed at
5 Hz.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>E3 &mdash; closed loop, full quadruped.</strong> A frozen Go1 policy is
deployed in MuJoCo at 50 Hz policy rate, 500 Hz physics, ten substeps per policy step, with the filter
inside the loop. Ten episodes of 500 steps are run at each of three commanded velocities (0.5 m/s,
1.0 m/s, and 0.3 m/s with turning) under matched random seeds and matched observation noise, giving
\(n = 30\) per scheme.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>E5 &mdash; spectral decomposition.</strong> The same closed-loop setup under
baseline ZOH only, over 30 rollouts across the three velocity commands. Mean torque per policy step is
logged at 50 Hz, with spectral splits at 5 Hz and 25 Hz and intra-step variance supplying the
&gt; 25 Hz component.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Predictive filter.</strong> An order-8 linear autoregressive model, AR(8), is
fitted by least squares to baseline rollouts and rolled forward two steps, so that a symmetric Hann
window can be applied without waiting for future samples and therefore without latency.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Load gate.</strong> Joints whose mean absolute torque \(|\bar{\tau}|\) falls
below the 3.2 N&middot;m crossover are filtered; the rest pass through unmodified.</p>

