<p class="noind">At \(\rho = 0.2\) an unloaded joint dissipates 10.9 times the heat while moving 1.8%
more; a joint carrying 20 N&middot;m dissipates 2% more heat for the same command. In the unloaded
condition dissipation follows \(\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_0 + \kappa\rho/(1-\rho)\) with \(\kappa =
38.4\) and a maximum residual of 0.45%. The crossover, where \(\bar{\tau}^2 = \mathrm{Var}(\tau)\),
lies at 3.2 N&middot;m.</p>

<p>Measured mean \(|\tau|\) per joint family on the Go1 is 1.24&ndash;1.48 N&middot;m for hip
abduction, 2.00&ndash;2.59 N&middot;m for the thighs, and 5.88&ndash;6.88 N&middot;m for the calves.
Eight of the twelve joints sit below the 3.2 N&middot;m threshold; the four knee joints sit above it.
This is why posture dominates knee dissipation while jitter dominates the non-weight-bearing joints,
and why the single most-cited piece of evidence for the jitter hypothesis is a G1 ankle-roll motor,
one of the least loaded joints on a humanoid leg.</p>

