<p class="noind">Removing the effort terms moves 25 percentage points of heat into the filter-reachable
band, from 18.1% to 43.3%, and inflates total dissipation by 47%. The predictive filter returns 47% and
54% of the reachable budget on the two policies. The raw saving differs by nearly a factor of three,
8.5% against 23.5%, and yet the recovered <em>fraction</em> is stable. This is the practically useful
form of the result: measure the band split of one baseline rollout, halve the reachable share, and you
have an estimate of the closed-loop saving before writing any filter code.</p>

<p>Recovery is not bounded by predictor accuracy. Closed-loop predictability falls from \(R^2 = 0.609\)
to 0.402 between the two policies, and the wasteful policy nonetheless gives up more of its heat. On
that policy, gating removes 12.1% of the heat for a 15% tracking penalty against the ungated filter's
23.5% reduction for a 59% penalty. Delayed filtering fails outright there: 37% falls overall, and every
episode at 1.0 m/s. The policy that most needs help is the one the obvious intervention hurts most.</p>

<h2><span class="secno">6.</span>Discussion and Practical Guidance</h2>

<p class="noind"><strong>Profile the spectral split before intervening.</strong> A single baseline
rollout establishes the reachable budget, and halving it estimates the attainable closed-loop recovery.
If the reachable share is small, command filtering cannot prevent thermal overload and the effort
belongs in the reward function or the posture instead.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Do not buy zero phase with delay.</strong> Filter latency is subtracted from
the policy's own stability margin, and the resulting falls appear only at speed. Filtering
architectures must be evaluated at the maximum commanded velocity, not the convenient one.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Gate by load.</strong> Restrict filtering to joints operating below
\(\bar{\tau} \approx \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\tau)}\). On the Go1 this preserved stability while recovering
most of the tracking fidelity the ungated filter gave away.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Prefer effort regularization to <code>action_rate</code>.</strong> Reward
formulations aimed at thermal safety should penalize joint torque and energy rather than discrete
action differences, which govern 8% of the transient magnitude against 89%.</p>

<h2><span class="secno">7.</span>Related Work</h2>

<p class="noind"><strong>Thermal-aware locomotion.</strong> Recent methods add motor temperature
observations and thermal reward bounds,<a class="cite" href="#ref2">[2]</a> or train residual policies
conditioned on thermal state.<a class="cite" href="#ref3">[3]</a> Contact-constrained inverse
kinematics has addressed multi-limbed humanoid thermal
redistribution.<a class="cite" href="#ref9">[9]</a> All require retraining; we characterize what a
post-hoc intervention on a frozen checkpoint can and cannot do.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Actuation interfaces.</strong> Related architectures include actuator reality
shaping via two-degree-of-freedom tracking controllers,<a class="cite" href="#ref10">[10]</a>
high-frequency linear feedback for torque interpolation,<a class="cite" href="#ref11">[11]</a>
voltage-realizable acceleration bounding,<a class="cite" href="#ref12">[12]</a> action chunk
optimization,<a class="cite" href="#ref13">[13]</a> B-spline policy
representations,<a class="cite" href="#ref14">[14]</a> and forward delay compensation
networks.<a class="cite" href="#ref15">[15]</a> Our contribution is the thermal-loss consequence of
these interfaces rather than a new one.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Smoothness regularization.</strong> CAPS,<a class="cite" href="#ref5">[5]</a>
Lipschitz-constrained networks<a class="cite" href="#ref16">[16]</a> and spectral
normalization<a class="cite" href="#ref17">[17]</a> suppress high-frequency oscillation, and a
benchmark study reports improved control smoothness at small performance
cost.<a class="cite" href="#ref18">[18]</a> None of this work measures physical electrical dissipation
or separates steady holding torque from command ripple on a legged system.</p>

<p class="noind"><strong>Control rate and discretization.</strong> Robust locomotion has been shown at
8 Hz on ANYmal C, with low-rate policies less sensitive to actuation
latency;<a class="cite" href="#ref19">[19]</a> Q-learning degenerates as the time step
shrinks;<a class="cite" href="#ref20">[20]</a> policies that choose their own action durations reduce
average control frequency without losing reward.<a class="cite" href="#ref21">[21]</a> None of it
reports a thermal or electrical quantity as a function of control rate.</p>

<h2><span class="secno">8.</span>Limitations</h2>

<p class="noind">All experiments are rigid-body simulation using mean squared torque
\(\mathbb{E}[\tau^2]\) as an electrical loss proxy under constant \(K_t\); iron losses, inverter
switching dissipation and winding thermal diffusion are omitted. The single-joint sweeps omit contact
dynamics entirely. Everything is measured on one quadruped platform on flat terrain, so the
cross-morphology claim the introduction leans on &mdash; that the G1 ankle-roll case is the same
phenomenon &mdash; is argued, not demonstrated. The 5 Hz gait split is a hand-chosen parameter based on
the 2 Hz stride fundamental and has not been swept. The reward ablations of Section 5.2 are single-seed
over 8 s intervals, with no confidence intervals reported anywhere in this draft
(<span class="pending">pending</span>, Appendix C). No hardware measurement supports the
3.2 N&middot;m crossover.</p>

<h2><span class="secno">9.</span>Conclusion</h2>

<p class="noind">Actuator overheating in learned legged locomotion decomposes into steady posture
torque and dynamic command ripple. Because the two combine in quadrature, command filtering offers
meaningful thermal relief only on lightly loaded joints below the crossover. In closed loop the phase
lag that smoothing introduces is taken directly out of the policy's stability margin, so causal
predictive filtering with load-dependent gating &mdash; not zero-phase smoothing &mdash; is what can
safely recover actuator thermal margin on a frozen checkpoint.</p>

